#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The optimizing tier's correctness lane on AWFY.

Runs each benchmark through the AWFY harness on the oracle and on bin/lucifer
in every JIT mode: the default (-X lucifer_jit=on), -X lucifer_jit=always,
and always with every 7th guard failing (-X lucifer_stress=deopt:7). Output
must match the oracle's line for line once the timings are masked, and in the
compiling modes the tier must have compiled and entered code (its
-X lucifer_jit_stats report), so that a lane cannot pass by not compiling.
Then lanes run a program each under -X lucifer_jit=always at two sizes and
require that a count of the tier's does not grow with the iterations:
  exits  tests/kernel/jit_exits.py, whose hot code holds instructions the
         tier does not compile: side exits. Code that keeps leaving at the
         same place stays in the interpreter instead of paying an entry and
         an exit each time.
  calls  tests/kernel/jit_calls.py, whose hot calls the tier can inline once
         it has feedback: entries into compiled code, direct calls and
         compiled calls.
         None of its functions may be disabled (recompiled until the tier
         gives up on it) or retired (an entry left to the interpreter).
  large  tests/kernel/jit_large_callees.py, whose hot call is of a function
         too large to inline whole but small along the path the program
         runs: entries into compiled code, direct and compiled calls.
  recursive tests/kernel/jit_recursive_budget.py, whose recursive descent
         keeps room for the calls that finish each level. One outlined
         recursive call per descent, with one descent per eight iterations.
  turns  tests/kernel/jit_gen_turns.py, whose hot generator a C consumer
         resumes, each turn calling a function its unit's budget left out:
         direct calls past the consumer's one step a turn. The call is in
         the loop the generator runs, and taken in line once hot.
  quiet  tests/kernel/jit_quiet_misses.py, whose hot lookups miss and whose
         hot generators end where CPython makes no exception: error exits.
  raised tests/kernel/jit_raised.py, whose hot code catches exceptions
         kernel builtins raise without a handler of their own (send() on a
         finished generator or coroutine, a dict miss, list.pop(), next(),
         an async generator's asend passing each value on through a
         cleanup handler): side exits and error exits the interpreter
         unwinds. The exception leaves compiled kernel code as its call's
         result, and kernel code raises one again without leaving.
  caught tests/kernel/jit_caught.py, whose hot code raises exceptions that
         handlers in the same compiled code take: error exits, and exits
         at a return for the frame object the traceback made.
  compiled  tests/kernel/jit_compiled_calls.py, whose hot calls are of
         functions it cannot inline: direct calls (compiled code entered
         from C). Compiled code makes them itself.
  ccalls tests/kernel/jit_c_calls.py, whose hot code makes C call compiled
         functions (a dict lookup calls its keys' __hash__ and __eq__):
         direct calls (compiled code entered through the eval loop's C
         entry). C enters the callee's unit itself, the kernel's
         call_special (which the comparison reaches, with *args) too.
  shapes tests/kernel/jit_call_shapes.py, whose hot calls are of functions
         it cannot inline with star, keyword and keyword-only parameters
         and cells, some made with keywords: entries into compiled code and
         direct calls. Each takes its callee's direct entry.
  kwcalls tests/kernel/jit_kw_calls.py, whose hot calls of functions,
         methods and classes it knows pass keywords: the calls made as C
         makes a keyword call (-X lucifer_jit_stats' call_kw). Each binds
         its keywords at compile time and inlines.
  bound  tests/kernel/jit_bound_calls.py, whose hot calls are of functions
         the site met (nested functions, closures, functions passed in),
         with keywords, defaults, keyword-only parameters and star
         arguments on either side: the calls made as C makes a keyword or
         star call (call_kw). Each binds at compile time, in line or not.
         Under -X lucifer_jit=on: the interpreter learns the callees first.
  cmethods tests/kernel/jit_class_methods.py, whose hot methods call
         classmethods and staticmethods through their class: entries into
         compiled code, direct and compiled calls. The loads bind at
         compile time and every call inlines.
  attrorder tests/kernel/jit_attr_order.py, whose hot loop reads and
         writes attributes of instances of one class that got their names
         in different orders: guard and side exits, and entries left to
         the interpreter while a unit's entry waits. Each instance keeps
         its values in line, where the class's sites read them.
  inlinedpoly tests/kernel/jit_inlined_poly_loads.py, whose hot method,
         inlined, loads attributes of instances of three classes: entries,
         direct and compiled calls, guard and side exits. The interpreter
         feeds the site's polymorphic cache, which the inline tests.
  super  tests/kernel/jit_super.py, whose hot constructors and methods go
         through super(): side exits. None of its functions may be retired.
  inline tests/kernel/jit_inline_paths.py, whose hot loops read and store
         list and tuple items the running thread owns, box ints and floats,
         and build small lists and tuples: the C paths of those operations
         (compiled code runs them in line).
  repeat the same program, whose hot loop repeats a list ([x] * n):
         entries into compiled code, direct and compiled calls (the copies
         are made without list.__mul__).
  classes  tests/kernel/jit_class_calls.py, whose hot loop calls classes
         with Python initializers: entries into compiled code, direct and
         compiled calls (the instance is made in line, __init__ inlines).
  objects  tests/kernel/jit_virtual_objects.py, whose hot loop makes
         instances and tuples nothing outside compiled code sees: the
         tuples and instances compiled code allocates (-X lucifer_jit_stats'
         allocation census). They stay virtual.
  iterators  tests/kernel/jit_virtual_iterators.py, whose hot loop runs
         loops over ranges, lists and tuples, some in inlined callees, and
         carries a tuple around a loop: the iterators and tuples compiled
         code allocates. Iterators only the stack holds and objects only
         phis carry stay virtual.
  carried  tests/kernel/jit_carried_objects.py, whose hot loop runs loops
         over enumerate, zip, map and filter objects: the ints, tuples,
         lists, dicts and instances compiled code allocates. An object
         whose fields a loop changes stays virtual round the loop, its
         count a number, and the tuples its steps return are unpacked
         without being made.
  fused  tests/kernel/jit_virtual_generators.py, whose hot loop runs for
         loops over generators it makes: the resumes the interpreter and
         compiled code run, and the ints and tuples compiled code
         allocates. The loop runs the generator's body in line: the
         generator never exists, and a tuple it yields is unpacked
         without being made.
  forward  tests/kernel/jit_forward_calls.py, whose hot code calls classes
         through type.__call__ and operators through call_special, which
         forward their arguments with f(a, *args, **kwargs): the tuples,
         lists and dicts compiled code allocates. The call passes them on.
  literalmethods tests/kernel/jit_literal_methods.py, whose hot loop calls
         a method on a freshly made list. The calls use its descriptor,
         avoiding helpers for lookup and binding.
  slices tests/kernel/jit_slices.py, whose hot loop slices strs, bytes,
         lists and tuples by bounds it computes: the ints compiled code
         allocates. The bounds stay unboxed (luc-14n0).
  guards tests/kernel/jit_guards.py, whose hot loop's guards check what a
         writer can change (a global, a function's code, a class
         attribute) or what never changes (the range iterator's type):
         the guards its units execute (-X lucifer_jit_stats' per-unit
         census). They move out of the loop.
  gens   tests/kernel/jit_gen_resumes.py, whose hot generators and
         coroutines are resumed by a for loop, next() and send(), a builtin
         consuming a generator expression, the first run of `yield from`
         and await: the resumes the interpreter runs (each resume enters
         compiled code).
  consumers tests/kernel/jit_gen_consumers.py, whose hot code hands
         generators to the builtins that consume them and steps generators
         it did not make with next() and send(): side exits, entries left
         to the interpreter and units retired. Compiled code resumes them.
  callees tests/kernel/jit_callee_calls.py, whose hot calls are of
         methods at a site that meets four classes, and of functions,
         bound methods and closures the code reaches through locals and
         attributes: calls compiled code makes of a callee it could not
         name or of a polymorphic site's join (-X lucifer_jit_stats'
         call_unknown and call_poly). The tier inlines them all.
  order  tests/kernel/jit_call_order.py in both orders (luc-sl4y): a loop
         calling a function through a local after, or before, another loop
         called it through a class attribute with other operand types:
         entries into compiled code, direct and compiled calls, and calls
         of callees it could not name. Each site inlines the callee.
  rebind tests/kernel/jit_rebind.py, whose hot functions read a global
         and rebind it on every call (luc-ffdo): guard and side exits, and
         entries left to the interpreter while a dead unit's entry waits.
         None of its functions may be disabled or retired.
  phases tests/kernel/jit_phases.py, whose hot function meets floats at
         one more site per phase, in more phases than a fixed recompile
         budget allows (luc-idah): guard and side exits, and entries left
         to the interpreter while a dead unit's entry waits. None of its
         functions may be disabled or retired.
  rare   tests/kernel/jit_rare_fails.py, whose hot loop's iterable is a
         tuple on one call in 600 (luc-tax0): helper calls compiled code
         makes, one per eight iterations for the tuple's calls, which run
         their loop outside the unit. A guard that fails that rarely costs
         less in exits than a build that gives up its site, which it keeps.
  seqkinds tests/kernel/jit_seq_kinds.py, whose hot loops compare tuples
         and lists of 40 items whose classes the kernel's comparison meets
         by the half dozen (luc-tax0): helper calls compiled code makes, a
         few per comparison and none per item. The comparison reads sizes
         and items in line under a guard on the kind its classes share.
  intrange tests/kernel/jit_int_range_prims.py: a hot loop steps a range
         iterator and multiplies integers. Allow nine helpers per iteration,
         two fewer than when both primitives run out of line.
  raresub tests/kernel/jit_rare_subclass.py, whose hot comparisons of 40
         items learned plain tuples and then meet a namedtuple now and then
         (luc-tax0), compiled as calls come (-X lucifer_jit=on): helper
         calls compiled code makes, about 27 an iteration for the loop's
         generic comparisons and none per item (35 when the comparison went
         generic). The sites that give up the exact class read sizes and
         items under a guard on the kind both classes share.
  typelookup tests/kernel/jit_type_lookup.py, whose hot loop looks up an
         inherited attribute and a missing name on a class supplied by
         the caller: helper calls compiled code makes.
  retry  tests/kernel/jit_retry.py, whose hot function leaves compiled code
         on every call for a while and then stops (luc-j4mj): calls
         compiled code makes of it, and entries left to the interpreter,
         while its entry waits. It is compiled and entered again.
  unpack tests/kernel/jit_unpack_generic.py, whose hot loop unpacks named
         tuples, a tuple subclass with its own __iter__ and a generator:
         side exits and entries left to the interpreter. The kernel's unpack
         is called from compiled code.
  contexts tests/kernel/jit_contexts.py, whose hot loop runs with
         statements: entries left to the interpreter and retired units.
         LOAD_SPECIAL compiles.
  marked tests/kernel/jit_marked.py, whose polymorphic attribute site went
         generic and still meets, now and then, an instance whose class's
         entry it cannot read in line: compiles and entries left to the
         interpreter. The exit costs an exit, not a recompile.
  shadowed tests/kernel/jit_shadowed.py, whose polymorphic method site
         meets an instance that shadows the method in its own dict:
         compiles and guard exits. The site stops leaving and recompiling.
  kept   tests/kernel/jit_marked_guards.py, whose hot loops negate floats
         and Fractions (also inside abs()) where the first compile guessed
         an int with no feedback: guard and side exits, and entries left to
         the interpreter. The unit built after the site is marked keeps no
         guard there.
  shifts tests/kernel/jit_wide_shift.py, whose array packing shifts past
         a signed word: guard exits and entries left to the interpreter.
  hoisted tests/kernel/jit_unpack_phases.py, whose hot loop unpacks a tuple
         and then a list, by turns, after the tuple's guard moved out of
         the loop: entries left to the interpreter and side exits. The
         code recompiles once, into a unit that takes both.
  megaiter tests/kernel/jit_mega_iter.py, whose hot loops run str.join and
         set() over a deque's iterator (a Python class) and a filter, 200
         items each, after their sites met more classes than their arms
         take: compiled calls, 24 an iteration for the loops' set-up, none
         per item. Each loop takes the classes it meets most in line.
  useriter tests/kernel/jit_user_iter_end.py, whose hot loops run over
         iterators of a user class that end by raising StopIteration:
         side and error exits. The raise leaves the inlined __next__ for
         the loop's end with its traceback entry made in place.
  reclaim tests/kernel/jit_reclaim.py, whose rounds each compile a
         function of their own and then kill its unit: the dead units the
         tier still holds at exit. They are freed once no activation is in
         them.
  boolpow tests/kernel/jit_bool_float_pow.py, whose hot loop raises floats
         to bools' powers and bools to floats': guard and side exits.
  fields tests/kernel/jit_unboxed_fields.py, whose hot loop keeps instances
         it made with a computed float field: float boxes. The instance is
         made real with the number stored unboxed.
  operators tests/kernel/jit_operator_arms.py, whose hot ==, !=, +, []
         and `in` meet operands of a few classes at each site: helper
         calls compiled code makes. Each site takes the classes it met in
         line, the operator's dispatch folded on each.
  truth  tests/kernel/jit_default_truth.py, whose hot loop tests instances
         with neither __bool__ nor __len__: generic truth operations.
  typed  tests/kernel/jit_typed_items.py, whose hot loops read and store
         bytearray items and array items of every numeric typecode: helper
         calls compiled code makes. Each item is read and stored in line.
  strs   tests/kernel/jit_str_chars.py, whose hot loops read str characters
         and compare strs with one character: helper calls compiled code
         makes. Each is read and compared in line.
  positional tests/kernel/jit_positional_args.py, whose hot calls omit
         optional positional arguments: tuples and lists compiled code
         allocates. The callers apply defaults directly.
A third lane allows one count per iteration:
  inlined  tests/kernel/jit_inlined_frames.py, whose hot method makes a call
         the tier cannot inline: direct and compiled calls, one per
         iteration for that callee, none for the method, which inlines.
The perf lane records Richards under -X perf with `perf record -g` and
requires that at least 95% of the samples in compiled code (code perf finds
in no file: it looks it up in /tmp/perf-PID.map) resolve to py:: symbols.
It reports SKIP where perf cannot record.
Exit status 1 on any difference.
"""
